WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.445 --> 00:00:03.945
(gentle orchestral music)

00:00:14.520 --> 00:00:17.010
- It was for me, pretty
difficult to find out

00:00:17.010 --> 00:00:19.890
how much I would emphasis on the emotion

00:00:19.890 --> 00:00:21.780
because you can destroy with music,

00:00:21.780 --> 00:00:23.433
very quickly, the intimacy.

00:00:25.260 --> 00:00:28.860
We recorded with a full
on 40 string orchestra

00:00:28.860 --> 00:00:32.040
and then I used the filter
that cut everything off.

00:00:32.040 --> 00:00:34.230
On top of that, we
could hear every breath.

00:00:34.230 --> 00:00:36.783
It was a little bit like the inner voice.

00:00:36.783 --> 00:00:41.043
(gentle orchestral music)

00:00:41.043 --> 00:00:42.180
(upbeat jazz music)

00:00:42.180 --> 00:00:45.570
- We knew we were gonna use
the lineup of a jazz band

00:00:45.570 --> 00:00:47.520
but we didn't want it
to feel like '20s jazz.

00:00:47.520 --> 00:00:50.340
So we talked about the
feeling of rock and roll.

00:00:50.340 --> 00:00:53.270
We wanted a lot of really
heavy driving riffs.

00:00:53.270 --> 00:00:56.250
(upbeat jazz music)

00:00:56.250 --> 00:01:00.510
And then we would give it to
unison horns or unison saxes

00:01:00.510 --> 00:01:03.348
and it would give this
real muscularity to it.

00:01:03.348 --> 00:01:06.431
(upbeat jazz music)

00:01:09.952 --> 00:01:12.390
(gentle music)

00:01:12.390 --> 00:01:13.230
- It still leaves the question,

00:01:13.230 --> 00:01:16.130
how much does the music want
to say about what's going on?

00:01:18.540 --> 00:01:21.840
I try not to get ahead of the
actors, of the characters,

00:01:21.840 --> 00:01:24.150
of what they're feeling and experiencing,

00:01:24.150 --> 00:01:26.310
but still treat it very subjectively

00:01:26.310 --> 00:01:27.693
from their point of view.

00:01:28.570 --> 00:01:32.070
(gentle orchestral music)

00:01:36.690 --> 00:01:38.250
- The laundry and taxes cue,

00:01:38.250 --> 00:01:40.380
which continues through that scene,

00:01:40.380 --> 00:01:43.110
that was one of the most
tricky scenes to score

00:01:43.110 --> 00:01:47.398
because it was so significant
a moment emotionally

00:01:47.398 --> 00:01:50.310
(dramatic orchestral music)

00:01:50.310 --> 00:01:52.770
that just called for a kind of effusive,

00:01:52.770 --> 00:01:56.163
like a giving in to the
melancholy and the sweetness.

00:01:57.101 --> 00:02:00.684
(playful orchestral music)

00:02:02.820 --> 00:02:04.860
- Composing for me is
really like sculpting.

00:02:04.860 --> 00:02:07.230
I mean, you really take
a chunk out of the stone,

00:02:07.230 --> 00:02:10.113
another chunk, and
finally it reveals itself.

00:02:12.990 --> 00:02:15.750
It isn't the music, it
isn't the orchestra,

00:02:15.750 --> 00:02:17.280
it isn't the composer.

00:02:17.280 --> 00:02:18.960
It is this interconnection

00:02:18.960 --> 00:02:22.773
of all of these elements
that makes music, music.

00:02:22.773 --> 00:02:25.856
(bright piano music)

00:02:32.345 --> 00:02:34.595
(no audio)

